A very clean next experiment would be a mini seat-reservation system. It lets you reproduce the exact database problem without needing PostgreSQL yet:

two users read the same valid state, both make a decision from it, then both attempt to commit.

I’d build it in three passes:

1. naive read → calculate → write
2. pessimistic locking with a mutex
3. optimistic concurrency with CAS

Start with one seat remaining and two users trying to reserve it.

1. Intentionally broken version

The barrier is important here because it forces both threads to read the same snapshot before either writes.

C
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int seats = 1;
int tickets_sold = 0;

pthread_barrier_t barrier;

void *reserve(void *arg) {
    char *name = arg;

    int snapshot = seats;

    printf("%s sees %d seat(s)\n", name, snapshot);

    pthread_barrier_wait(&barrier);

    if (snapshot > 0) {
        seats = snapshot - 1;
        tickets_sold++;

        printf("%s bought a ticket\n", name);
    }

    return NULL;
}

int main(void) {
    pthread_t a, b;

    pthread_barrier_init(&barrier, NULL, 2);

    pthread_create(&a, NULL, reserve, "A");
    pthread_create(&b, NULL, reserve, "B");

    pthread_join(a, NULL);
    pthread_join(b, NULL);

    printf("\nSeats remaining: %d\n", seats);
    printf("Tickets sold:    %d\n", tickets_sold);

    pthread_barrier_destroy(&barrier);
}

You can get conceptually:

A sees 1 seat
B sees 1 seat

A bought a ticket
B bought a ticket

Seats remaining: 0
Tickets sold:    2

Which is beautiful because:

seats == 0

looks completely reasonable!

But your invariant was:

tickets_sold + seats_initially_remaining <= 1

and you've sold two copies of one seat.

That's exactly the kind of thing we were discussing with database application logic. The final shared variable doesn't even necessarily look corrupted.

Then make version 2.

2. Pessimistic concurrency

Don't protect just the assignment.

Protect the entire logical operation:

C
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void *reserve(void *arg) {
    char *name = arg;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (seats > 0) {
        printf("%s sees %d seat(s)\n", name, seats);

        seats--;
        tickets_sold++;

        printf("%s bought a ticket\n", name);
    } else {
        printf("%s found no seats\n", name);
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    return NULL;
}

Now you're implementing:

LOCK

observe state
verify invariant
calculate
modify state

UNLOCK

One user gets:

1 → 0

and the other sees:

0

This is basically:

SQL
SELECT ...
FOR UPDATE;

territory.

3. Then make it optimistic

This is where I think it'll become really satisfying given everything you've already done with CAS.

Instead of preventing both users from reasoning simultaneously, let them.

C
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <stdio.h>

atomic_int seats = 1;
atomic_int tickets_sold = 0;

pthread_barrier_t barrier;

void *reserve(void *arg) {
    char *name = arg;

    int expected = atomic_load(&seats);

    printf("%s sees %d seat(s)\n", name, expected);

    pthread_barrier_wait(&barrier);

    if (expected <= 0) return NULL;

    int desired = expected - 1;

    if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&seats, &expected, desired)) {
        atomic_fetch_add(&tickets_sold, 1);
        printf("%s bought the ticket\n", name);
    } else {
        printf("%s failed: state changed underneath them (now %d)\n",
            name, expected);
    }

    return NULL;
}

Now both can do:

A reads 1
B reads 1

Both independently conclude:

"I can change 1 → 0"

Then A might execute:

CAS(1 → 0)

success

B then attempts:

CAS(1 → 0)

expected 1
actual   0

FAIL

And remember the convenient property of C's CAS:

C
atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&seats, &expected, desired);

on failure updates expected with reality.

So B literally gets told:

Your assumption:
1 seat

Reality:
0 seats

That's almost a miniature optimistic database transaction.

And then I'd add one more version after you've got those working, because it'll make the database analogy much stronger.

Instead of:

C
atomic_int seats;

make the state something like:

C
struct BookingState {
    int seats;
    int price;
    int version;
};

Imagine the user's decision depends on multiple pieces of state:

if seats > 0 AND price <= $100:
    buy

Now you can't safely CAS just seats, because your decision was based on:

seats
AND
price

Maybe you saw:

version 7

seats = 1
price = $80

and while you're calculating somebody changes the price:

version 8

seats = 1
price = $150

Your decision is stale even though seats never changed.

That leads naturally to the database-style pattern:

read:
    state + version

calculate privately

commit only if:
    version still equals what I read

Conceptually:

C
if (current_version == snapshot_version) {
    commit();
} else {
    retry();
}

which is almost exactly:

SQL
UPDATE booking
SET ...
WHERE id = 7
AND version = 42;

If it updates zero rows:

your snapshot lost the race

and you retry.

So I think seat reservation → mutex → CAS → multi-field versioned state would be an excellent next progression.

It takes the stuff you've already learned:

mutex
atomic
CAS
stale reads
immutability
versions

and turns them into what is effectively a tiny transaction engine.

