The lost races field is particularly interesting.

In the pessimistic version it should effectively be:

lost races: 0

because nobody is allowed to inspect the protected ticket while another thread is claiming one.

In the optimistic version you may see:

Thread A: ticket 583 is FREE
Thread B: ticket 583 is FREE

A CAS: success
B CAS: FAILED

and races increases.

Yet both should finish with:

tickets sold: 100000

with every ticket index itself serving as a unique ticket number.

And now I think you'll see the conceptual difference much more clearly.

The pessimistic implementation says:

                 GLOBAL LOCK
                     |
        +------------+------------+
        |            |            |
     ticket 12    ticket 500   ticket 90000

Even completely unrelated ticket claims pass through that one lock.

The optimistic one says:

ticket 12        ticket 500        ticket 90000
   CAS              CAS                CAS
    |                |                  |
 independent     independent        independent

So only people actually fighting over the same state need to conflict.

But here's the part I'd really pay attention to when you benchmark it: I would not assume the optimistic version always wins.

Try:

C
#define THREADS 2

then:

C
#define THREADS 4
#define THREADS 8
#define THREADS 16
#define THREADS 32

You may discover something like:

low/moderate contention:
optimistic does very well

extreme contention:
CAS failures/retries explode
mutex becomes competitive or even faster

And that would take us directly into the real engineering question:

At what contention level does optimism stop paying?

There’s also a delicious cache issue hiding in this program that you've already learned enough to predict: adjacent owner[] elements share cache lines, so even threads claiming different ticket IDs can still interfere through false sharing. That gives us a natural follow-up experiment once you've run this version.

