Absolutely. This time we're going to make the failure emerge from genuine concurrent operations, rather than just hardcoding an incorrect transaction and asking you to detect it.

And I want to preserve your opportunity to solve it, so I'll give you the flawed implementation, the intended invariant, and what to look for — but not the corrected code.

Experiment: Two competing transactions, four replicas

Our system starts with one account:

Available balance

$500

Transaction A

−$300

Pay Alice

Transaction B

−$400

Pay Bob

Both transactions are individually valid against the original $500 balance, but cannot both be honored.

Our four replicas are R0, R1, R2, and R3. The first three are supposed to follow the voting protocol. R3 is Byzantine and can endorse conflicting transactions deliberately.

Each replica must vote on one transaction for the same log position. A proposal needs three of the four replicas to approve it before being considered committed.

The desired safety property is that A and B must never both be finalized for that position.

