What makes this different from our first attempt?

The Byzantine replica is still deliberately faulty, but the three honest replicas now have a real concurrency bug.

Two independent proposer threads are simultaneously calling:

C
request_vote(replica_id, proposal);

Each honest replica has shared state:

C
atomic_int voted_for;

And we've introduced our familiar pthread_barrier to force the two competing requests to overlap at an interesting point.

Notice that I'm already using atomics. This is intentional: the individual reads and writes are data-race-free in C, but that doesn't necessarily mean the entire voting operation is atomic.

When you run the program, you should see an outcome resembling:

--- REPLICA VOTES ---
R0: A=YES B=YES
R1: A=YES B=YES
R2: A=YES B=YES
R3: A=YES B=YES

--- CONSENSUS RESULTS ---
A: 4 votes -> COMMIT
B: 4 votes -> COMMIT

SAFETY FAILURE: conflicting transactions finalized!
Balance if both honored: $-200

We have produced two conflicting commit decisions for the same log position. The negative balance illustrates what could happen downstream if both payment claims were honored.

Your debugging challenge

I would focus on two distinct questions.

First: local synchronization. How could an honest replica apparently vote for both proposals when voted_for is already an atomic_int? Can you modify the voting operation so an honest replica approves at most one proposal per log position?

Second: collective agreement. Once an honest replica cannot accidentally vote twice, how does requiring three approvals from four replicas protect the system even when R3 intentionally approves both?

An important constraint: you cannot make R3 trustworthy simply by introducing a mutex. It is a Byzantine participant, meaning it may deliberately disregard the protocol. Also, be careful about putting our two-thread barrier inside an exclusive lock — that can produce a deadlock.

This is still a simplified BFT-inspired simulator, not a complete distributed consensus protocol. Each replica is modeled as local state accessed by concurrent request handlers, rather than as an independently secured machine. We're deliberately isolating one consensus safety problem before introducing networking and leader changes.

The goal is to discover why local synchronization and distributed agreement are two different layers of correctness.

