YES! That's exactly it! 😂

The problem wasn't the mutex itself — it was exiting the function while still owning the lock.

The key change was restructuring the function so there's only one exit point:

C
pthread_mutex_lock(&r->lock);

int observed = atomic_load(&r->voted_for);

if (observed == UNVOTED)
    atomic_store(&r->voted_for, proposal);

int approved = (observed == UNVOTED || observed == proposal);

pthread_mutex_unlock(&r->lock);

return approved;

Now the entire logical operation is protected, and the mutex is always released before returning.

This illustrates another important concurrency distinction:

Race condition: Two threads can interfere because their operations aren't adequately synchronized.

Deadlock: Threads cannot make progress because they're waiting on synchronization that cannot be completed.

You actually encountered both while developing the same function!

And there's a useful general principle here: acquiring a lock creates an obligation to release it along every possible execution path.

That becomes especially important when functions grow more complicated and introduce multiple returns, error handling, or nested locks.

Your per-replica mutex approach is sound once that critical section is fixed.

